course: everyday
lesson: 28
title: Farm animals and horses
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Farm animals are big, strong and easily frightened, and most are prey animals whose first idea when scared is to run, kick or push.
explain: A horse has eyes on the sides of its head, so it sees almost all around, but it has a blind spot right behind it and just in front of its nose; if something surprises it from behind, it may kick hard.
explain: That is why you never walk behind a horse: approach from the side towards its shoulder, talk softly so it knows you are there, and feed a treat only from a flat open hand.
explain: Cows with young calves can be very protective; walkers in fields should keep away from calves, close gates behind them and never walk between a cow and her calf.
explain: After touching farm animals, at a petting farm or anywhere, always wash your hands with soap and water, because animals can carry germs such as E. coli that make people very ill.
explain: Walkers in England crossed a field with cows and their calves while their dog ran ahead; the cows charged and knocked one of the walkers down, injuring him.
explain: A girl in Kentucky walked right behind a horse without speaking; it was startled, kicked out and bruised her leg badly.
fact: Never walk behind a horse, because it may kick if surprised.
fact: A horse cannot see directly behind itself or right in front of its nose.
fact: Approach a horse from the side, near its shoulder, and talk softly as you come.
fact: Feed a horse a treat from a flat, open hand so your fingers are not bitten by mistake.
fact: Always wear a riding helmet when you ride a horse or pony.
fact: Cows with calves can charge people and dogs to protect their young.
fact: Never walk between a cow and her calf.
fact: If cattle chase a walker with a dog, the walker should let go of the lead so both can get away.
fact: Always close farm gates behind you so animals cannot escape.
fact: Wash your hands with soap and water after touching farm animals.
fact: Farm animals can carry germs, such as E. coli, that can make people very ill.
fact: Do not climb into pens or fields with animals unless the farmer says you may.
fact: Cows with calves can be very protective and can trample people.
fact: A horse's kick is strong enough to break bones.
exercise: Why should you never walk behind a horse? | It may kick if it is surprised | Horses always bite from behind | It will follow you everywhere | Horses do not like the smell of children
exercise: Where can a horse not see? | Directly behind it and right in front of its nose | To its left side | To its right side | Up in the sky
exercise: How should you go up to a horse? | From the side towards its shoulder, talking softly | From behind, very quietly, so you do not frighten it at all | Running straight at its face | Jumping out to surprise it
exercise: How should you give a horse an apple? | On a flat, open hand | Held between two fingers | Thrown gently at its face | Held tight in your fist
exercise: What must you always wear to ride a pony? | A riding helmet | A bobble hat to keep warm | A pair of sunglasses | A baseball cap worn backwards
exercise: Why are cows with young calves dangerous to walkers? | They may charge to protect their calves | They want to eat the walkers' packed lunches and snacks | They like to chase people for fun | They cannot see people at all
exercise: You walk across a field in the Lake District. Where should you never go? | Between a cow and her calf | Along the edge of the field | Over the stile by the farm gate | Back along the way you came
exercise: Why should you close a farm gate behind you? | So the animals cannot get out | To keep the wind out | Because gates rust when they are left open | To stop the grass growing
exercise: What should you do straight after stroking goats at a petting farm? | Wash your hands with soap and water | Eat your lunch at once | Wipe your hands on your jeans until they look clean | Lick your fingers clean
exercise: Why must you wash your hands after touching farm animals? | They can carry germs like E. coli | The animals' fur is poisonous to touch | It stops animals from smelling you | Animals get upset if you don't wash
exercise: Why do horses and cows often run when scared? | They are prey animals and escape danger by running | They want to play chase | They are hunting | They are looking for food and fresh grass in another field
exercise: Can you climb into a pen with a bull to say hello? | No, only go into pens when the farmer says so | Yes, bulls love visitors | Yes, if you move very fast and keep out of its way | Yes, if you bring it some food
exercise: Why does talking softly help when you come near a horse? | It lets the horse know where you are so it is not startled | Horses understand every word | It makes the horse fall asleep | Horses can only hear quiet voices
test: Grace in Kentucky wants to walk round her pony to get to the gate. Which way is safest? | Round the front, or well clear of its back legs | Close behind it, touching its tail so it knows you are there | Under its belly | Straight behind it, very fast
test: A boy holds a carrot between his fingertips for a horse. What could go wrong? | The horse might nip his fingers by mistake | The horse cannot see carrots | The carrot will make the horse kick out at the boy | Horses never eat carrots
test: Which is true? | A horse cannot see what is directly behind it | A horse can see all the way round, even behind | Horses never kick | Horses always see in front of their nose
test: A family walking in Devon finds cows with calves in the field. Their dog is on a lead. What is wise? | Walk calmly round the edge, keeping well away from the calves | Walk right through the middle of the herd | Let the dog run to chase the cows | Stop to stroke the calves
test: Jacob leaves the gate open after crossing a sheep field. What could happen? | The sheep could escape onto the road | The sheep would stay put for good | The grass would stop growing | Nothing ever happens with open gates
test: At a petting farm in Ohio, Lily eats her sandwich straight after feeding the lambs. Why is this risky? | Germs from the lambs could get into her mouth | Lambs get jealous of sandwiches | Bread goes bad after you have been holding wool | The farm will run out of food
test: Why might a horse kick if you creep up behind it silently? | It is startled by something it cannot see | It wants to play with you | Horses kick everyone they meet, even from the front | It is trying to say hello
test: What should you do before going into a field or pen with animals? | Ask the farmer if it is safe | Climb the fence to see better | Shout so the animals come over | Bring food to throw over to them
test: horse at the farm, can i go round the back of it | No, it may kick if surprised | Yes, horses like that | Yes, if you're quick | Yes, pull its tail gently so it knows
test: giving the pony a carrot, how | On a flat open hand | Holding the very end in your fingers | Pushed into its mouth | Throw it at its face
test: cows with calves in the field, we've got the dog | If they chase, let go of the lead | Hold the dog tight and run for it | Walk between a cow and calf | Go and pat the calves
test: just patted the lambs, sandwich now? | Wash your hands with soap first | Yes, they looked clean | Just wipe them on your jeans first | Use the lamb's water
test: field's full of cows with babies, short cut through? | Go round another way if you can | Run through the middle fast | Walk close to look at the calves | Throw sticks so they move
test: wanna walk behind the horse to get my bag | Go round wide or speak and touch so it knows you are there | Walk right behind it quietly | Run past quickly | Pull its tail so it moves
test: cows are running at us and our dog!! | Let go of the dog and get out of the field | Hold the dog tight and stand completely still | Run towards the cows | Hide behind the dog
test: my uncle says a horse kicked a lad clean over a fence cos he walked right behind it | Real lesson: never walk behind a horse; «clean over a fence» may be exaggerated | Real lesson: horses only kick boys | It proves horses should never be ridden | It is gossip, so walking behind horses is fine
test: they reckon cows are harmless, they just eat grass, so it's fine to walk the dog through the field with calves | Not safe; cows with calves can charge people with dogs, so keep away from calves | They are right, cows never charge | They are right, if you walk between cow and calf | They are right, as long as the dog barks
test: Molly fell off her pony at the show and her hat came off, everyone's sharing the pic | Do not share it; she may be hurt or embarrassed, and it is her business | Share it, because falls are funny | Share it, so she practises harder | Share it, but crop out the pony
exam: Identify the region from which a horse ought to be approached. | From the side, near the shoulder, whilst speaking softly | From directly behind, so as not to alarm it with one's face | From directly in front of its nose | From beneath its neck without warning
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner of offering a treat to a horse? | Upon a flat and open hand, so that the fingers are not bitten by mistake | Between the fingertips, held close to the mouth | Thrown upon the ground beneath the horse | Held in a closed fist until the horse sniffs it
exam: It being given that cattle pursue a walker accompanied by a dog, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | The walker should release the lead so that both may escape | The walker should pick up the dog and stand still | The walker should run between the cattle and their calves | The walker should hold the lead tightly and face the cattle
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established practice concerning farm gates. | Gates should invariably be closed after passing through them | Gates should be left open to allow animals to move freely about | Gates should be climbed over rather than opened | Gates are of no concern to visitors to a farm
